-- Batch 12: source-sensitive editorial corrections to foundations, stars and historical medical vocabulary.
-- Grounded in Abū Maʿshar (I.2–3), Bonatti (First Tractate), al-Bīrūnī §§121,157 and Lilly II pp. 266,268,290–291.
-- No clinical recommendations, formula schemes, pre-existing historical interpretations, aliases or references altered.
PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON;

UPDATE terms SET short_definition='In Lilly’s historical medical astrology, a crisis is a marked alteration during an illness which he interpreted as a turning point toward recovery or worsening; this is a premodern prognostic category, not a modern clinical diagnosis.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: source-bounded historical distinction; not contemporary medical advice.' WHERE slug='medical-crisis';
UPDATE term_articles SET intro='Abū Maʿshar treats astrology as one branch of the science of the stars: it judges the indications of celestial conditions for the lower world, while a companion branch studies celestial structure and motions. Bonatti''s translated labels for the contemplative and practical branches are not always arranged in the same way, so his terminology must remain source-attributed.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: concise non-redundant source-attributed introduction.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='astrology');
UPDATE term_articles SET intro='The Sun, Moon, Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn form the seven moving celestial significators of the classical astrological system. This is the inherited astrological classification: in modern astronomical terminology the Sun is a star and the Moon is Earth''s natural satellite.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: concise non-redundant source-attributed introduction.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='seven-planets');
UPDATE term_articles SET intro='A testimony is an individual astrological indication used as evidence within a judgment. It can support or conflict with other indications, but it is not itself the final judgment.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: concise non-redundant source-attributed introduction.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='testimony');
UPDATE term_articles SET intro='Traditional planetary gender classifies Saturn, Jupiter, Mars and the Sun as masculine, Venus and the Moon as feminine, and Mercury as variable according to circumstances. This is distinct from the masculine/feminine series of zodiacal signs.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: concise non-redundant source-attributed introduction.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='planetary-gender');
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='The two sciences and differing Latin usage',body='Abū Maʿshar places celestial dimensions, sphere motions, planetary order and eclipses in the astronomical branch he calls the Science of the Universe. The other branch judges the indications of those conditions in the lower world (*Great Introduction*, I.2). Bonatti sometimes labels the contemplative mathematical branch ''astrology'' and the practical judgmental branch ''astronomy'' (*Liber Astronomiae*, First Tractate, XI–XIV). Their subject distinction should be described without forcing identical English labels onto both authors.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: revised actual explanation, preserving source distinctions and medical history context.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='astrology') AND section_key='astronomy-distinction';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='A claim about action, not just a sign',body='For Abū Maʿshar, celestial power concerns how motions and heavenly bodies are understood to alter the four mutable elements and thereby affect generation and corruption in the lower world (*Great Introduction*, I.3). His distinct doctrine of indication concerns what a celestial condition signifies. These are related explanatory claims, not synonymous terms or instructions for a separate chart calculation.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: revised actual explanation, preserving source distinctions and medical history context.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='celestial-power') AND section_key='causal-category';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='Al-Bīrūnī''s concentric sphere sequence',body='Al-Bīrūnī (§121) sets out seven planetary spheres from the terrestrial centre: Moon, Mercury, Venus, Sun, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn, followed by the sphere of the fixed stars. This is the inherited geocentric model, not the modern physical arrangement of Solar System orbits. It explains why astrological works refer to a planet as higher or lower in the ordering without implying greater strength or goodness in every chart.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: revised actual explanation, preserving source distinctions and medical history context.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='planetary-order') AND section_key='model-status';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='Lilly''s lunar distances, not fixed calendar dates',body='In his medical judgments Lilly follows the Moon from its position when illness began, looking at changes near 45°, 90° and 135° of separation and at later significant contacts (*Christian Astrology*, Book II, printed p. 266). The Moon''s daily motion is not uniform, so these angular checkpoints cannot be converted uncritically into fixed seventh- or fourteenth-day calendar rules. Lilly also interprets the planets the Moon meets at those stages; this is a historical predictive procedure, not medical guidance.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: revised actual explanation, preserving source distinctions and medical history context.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='critical-days') AND section_key='lilly-correction';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='An illness beginning and a stated fallback',body='Lilly prefers the Moon''s corrected position for the hour when the patient first took to bed. If that moment cannot be obtained, he allows the hour when the physician was first asked for a judgment (*Christian Astrology*, Book II, printed p. 266). Elsewhere he prefers the moment illness first invaded the patient and provides other consultation fallbacks (pp. 290–291). The exact root moment is therefore an attributed rule, not a single timeless convention attached to the English word ''decumbiture''.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: revised actual explanation, preserving source distinctions and medical history context.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='decumbiture') AND section_key='moment';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='Historical magnitude is an observational class',body='Al-Bīrūnī (§157) reports a catalogue arranging fixed stars by six magnitude classes and locating them in zodiacal longitude and ecliptic latitude. Such magnitude classes are observational identifications, not measurements of a star''s physical size. Modern apparent-magnitude numbers can aid comparison, but a modern photometric value should not be presented as the exact numerical scale of the medieval catalogue.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: revised actual explanation, preserving source distinctions and medical history context.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='fixed-star') AND section_key='brightness';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='Six historical classes are not modern photometry',body='Al-Bīrūnī (§157) distinguishes first through sixth magnitudes while describing the positional fixed-star catalogue. The ordinal classes describe apparent stellar prominence and support recognition of catalogued stars. The modern numerical magnitude scale retains the inverse brightness direction but uses a defined logarithmic photometric relation; the two should not be equated as identical measuring procedures.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: revised actual explanation, preserving source distinctions and medical history context.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='stellar-magnitude') AND section_key='scale';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='Bodily correspondences in a medical text',body='Lilly introduces the text he attributes to Hermes on decumbiture with the familiar zodiacal sequence beginning at the head under Aries (*Christian Astrology*, Book II, printed p. 268). That source is one historical presentation of zodiacal melothesia, not a complete account of all planetary and other body correspondences. The entries record what these authors believed and do not establish a contemporary mechanism, diagnosis or treatment.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: revised actual explanation, preserving source distinctions and medical history context.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='melothesia') AND section_key='historical-scope';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='The ninth and tenth illustrate two different classifications',body='The ninth place is cadent but has its own historically assigned topics, while the tenth is angular and also has distinct topics. Calling one cadent and the other angular classifies their relation to the chart''s pivots; it does not define their topics or determine the moral character of what happens there. Accidental strength and topical assignment remain different kinds of judgment.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: revised actual explanation, preserving source distinctions and medical history context.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='twelve-places') AND section_key='not-topic';
UPDATE term_article_sections SET heading='Bonatti''s natural-philosophical claim',body='Bonatti''s discourse on astral influence belongs to a natural-philosophical account of heavenly causes acting on terrestrial things (*Liber Astronomiae*, First Tractate). It is not a separate predictive technique or a criterion such as sect, aspect or dignity. His translated terminology can be compared with Abū Maʿshar''s Celestial Power without pretending that the expressions were one historically fixed lexical item.',editorial_notes='Batch 12: revised actual explanation, preserving source distinctions and medical history context.' WHERE term_id=(SELECT id FROM terms WHERE slug='astral-influence') AND section_key='causal-language';
